Date: 19 Mar 94 06:38:00 EST
Subject: [#94-3-234] BRAIN: Most Extropic Entity?

Are brains the most extropic entity?

At least in our solar system they are.  Especially when there are lots of them
all working together towards increasing extropy.  Consensual purpose within
society is more extropic than individual purpose, but consensual purpose
originates within individual purpose.

Steve Whitrow wrote 3/14/94: <I'm proposing that there is a meme or meme-complex
for the whole concept of extropianism, and the words "Extropian", etc are
represented either by separate memes or by part of the extropianism
meme-complex.> and <But for memetic evolution to occur independently of genetic
evolution / posthuman engineering, it clearly has to take place in RAM-type
memory, as I mentioned in the excerpt below.>

The extropian meme-complex goes back to Heraclitus/Empedocles, Aristotle's
"entelechy" as adopted by biologist Hans Driesch, Henri Bergson's "elan vital"
to early 20th century physicist's concept of "synergy" and Erwin Schrodinger's
1943 concept of life processes as "negentropy."  Arthur Koestler and Bucky
Fuller popularized this whole conceptual thrust in the '60s and '70s (with help
from Ilya Prigogine's dissipative structures) and voila, it turns into "extropy"
in the '80s.

Biological evolution has produced teleology at the individual level of
selection.  It is a selectional system based on the purpose of survival, as in,
survival of the fittest.  Fittest refers to survival.  Memetic evolution is a
way of describing a more complex selectional system that has come into being
with the development of higher consciousness in homo sapiens.  The new
selectional system is the ability of humans to select first-person subjective
purposes besides mere survival.  These purposes are the foundation of all our
knowledge, rationality, morality, values, and meaning.  Concepts (language) are
formed in consensual reality ("We") and enter into a memetic evolutionary
selection ecosystem of cooperation and/or competition just as genes do in
biological evolution.  Survival of the fittest concepts refers to the purposes
they originated in.  The purposes themselves ultimately either increase entropy
or decrease it within local regions of the universe (local being a matter of
perspective).  There appears to be no teleology in the universe as a whole
because the average density of entropy does not change, but local teleology can
arise in non-organic and memetic evolution (extropy) just as local teleology has
arisen in biological evolution (survival) and immune system evolution (somatic
selection to screen out "non-self").  Memetic evolution takes place in society
(consensual reality, the origin of meaning and knowledge), much of it in oral
histories, universities, museums and libraries.  When all human ideas are placed
into cyberspace for all of our access, memetic evolution will proceed at a
frenetic pace just as biological evolution has accelerated continuously until we
almost have the ability to biologically evolve anything we like any way we like.
The difference is that biological evolution only was directed towards one
purpose, survival of the fittest.  How many purposes is memetic evolution
directed towards?  Any limit?  Talk about complexity!

