Has anyone come up with a list of these based on experimental
evidence?  I don't trust anything based on introspection here, because
by the time we start introspecting we're way too old to remember
whether we learned something as a baby or inherited it.

> What is more plausible is the idea of someone who believed that
> noncontradiction applied in everyday circumstances, but not to specific
> "special" circumstances.

What about someone who believed that A <=> ~A was wrong, but had no
concept of transitivity?  I find this combination pretty plausible.

>  There is however something about the evolved-by-natural-selection
> structure of our brains which at least damps the variation in
> initial sets of plausible criticisms.

I think the set of criticisms can be changed too quickly to allow the
new ones to be criticized by the old ones--for example in a religious
conversion, or under intense external pressure.  At this point, it
doesn't matter what the initial set was--all bets are off, and the
person is starting out with a new "initial set."

> Even lower mammals have an approximate noncontradiction
> heuristic wired into their brains, as well as other information-
> manipulation structures each of which can provide a basis for a critical
