From: price@price.demon.co.uk (Michael Clive Price)
Date: Sun, 20 Mar 94 20:07:53 GMT
Subject: [#94-3-239] SCI: Inflation (was: PCR: Causality (Creation ...

I said:

>> the size of bound systems like atomic electron orbits and galactic
>> radii etc, in common with the speed of light and other constants, 
>> do not inflate with the Universe.

Steve Whitrow asks a tough question:

> The part I find unexplained is how these bound systems 'know' that
> their radii shouldn't inflate even when the space they occupy is
> inflating or expanding.

I can't think of a simple and convincing way of answering this, much
beyond saying "well they don't inflate and that's that!"

Here are my unconvincing reasons:

Looking at small systems, like atoms, we find that electron orbit radii
are determined by the relationships between various constants of nature,
like the speed of light, fine structure constant, planck's constant and
so on.  If their radii increased some of the constants would have to
alter too, and we don't detect changes (or haven't yet).  Earlier we saw
that it was the ability to measure distances by gauging constants (like
speed of light) that let us see space had/is expanding.  In the end it
is these constants that "prevent" atomic dimensions from inflating as
well.

We could turn the argument around and say that because we _do_ notice
the expansion of the universe via the Red shift and so forth that we can
conclude that the various "constants" are _not_ changing in some
complicated tandem arrangement that allows orbits and radii to increase
in step with the universe.

Very large, loosely bound systems (like superclusters of galaxies) _do_
expand (to some extent).  A galactic radius is in an equilibrium with
the Hubble expansion, in the sense that were the H expansion to suddenly
halt then its radius (or rather the orbital radii of the stars) would
contract slightly.  If they didn't energy wouldn't be conserved.  Same
true for an atom, but _very_ much less so.

