From: mnr@cs.cmu.edu
Date: Mon, 21 Mar 1994 20:43-EST
Subject: [#94-3-247] Steganography and Copyright on the printed page

> I found a paper called "Electronic Marking and Identification
> Techniques to Discourasge Document Copying" available via anonymous
> FTP from research.att.com, file "dist/brassil/infocom94.ps".

Yeah, I just went to a talk of his.  Sounds OK...involves small changes
to the whitespace between lines of text or between words.  He was mostly
interested in whether small changes would be preserved after photocopying
(answer: even 1 pixel shifts at 300dpi are still detectable after 10 copies).

None of their work attempts to counter a concerted attack which might
involve wholesale reformatting of the document...they were happy just
to put "enough of an obstacle" in the way of carefree copycats.  But
if you scan in such a document, for instance, all the markings are lost.

I'm personally more interested in methods of tagging ASCII documents in
such a way that even determined thieves can't rip out the tags.  For
instance, if you're willing to make small changes to the content of the
document, such as substituting a word for its synonym, such changes
can't be ripped out by a simple "smoothing" procedure.  If the thieves
can get several tagged copies of the document and compare them,
however, they may still defeat the scheme.  I've done some thinking and
experimenting on this (results: tagging is practical against all but
the most sophisticated thieves, but in the limit, if the thieves get n
copies of the document, you need a number of tags which is exponential
in n).  Send mail if you'd like to see the details.

-- Marc Ringuette (mnr@cs.cmu.edu).  Freely forward/archive this.


p.s. Don't take this to mean that I think stealing information is a bad thing!

